|~ The command line: its topic, `::cli`, and the names it declares under
   the kinds of their subjects, `table` under `::vec` and the names of
   the streams under `::any`. Their primitives take the values alone, the
   served subject and the values of their inputs, but that of `tap`, which
   reads the state of its call.
   The rules it holds: [QSHST#effect], [QSDSP#residence], [QSCLI#repl],
   [QSCLI#stdin]. ~|

:cli
  |~~ The command line runs a query on what a shell hands it and prints
      the answer: `qlang 'get /users | count' < data.json` in a script, and
      `qlang -i` a session of cells. It is the host a reader meets first,
      and the verbs it adds read and write the streams of the process.

      Usage: `qlang [--color=MODE] [--fail] '<query>'` runs a
      query, `qlang -i ['<query>']` opens a session of cells, the query
      its first, `qlang -h` prints this page and `qlang -V` the version.
      The flags stand before the query, one argument the shell quotes
      whole. `--color=auto`, the default, paints where stdout is a
      terminal, `always` and `never` whatever it is, and `NO_COLOR=1`
      and `FORCE_COLOR=1` turn `auto` to never and to always.

      A query runs on what stdin holds. JSON is read as values, an object
      a map whose keys are keywords, `{"name": "Ann"}` arriving as
      `{:name "Ann"}`; any other text is one string. A query headed by
      `in` reads the text as it came, JSON text among it, and
      `in | parseJson` reads it as JSON alone. A query that
      reads no subject, `qlang '::vec | doc'` or `qlang '[1 2] | sum'`,
      runs at once and reads no stdin. One that reads it with nothing
      piped, a terminal or `< /dev/null`, runs on the noun of the
      language, `::qlang`, so `qlang 'doc'` reads the first page; where a
      shell leaves stdin open, it waits for the end, as every reader of a
      pipe does.

      The answer is printed as the language prints it, whatever the data
      was: a map as `{:name "Ann" :tags #["a"]}`, a kind as `::map`. A
      string goes out as its text, so a query that ends in a conversion
      writes what the conversion made: `| json` the JSON text of the
      answer, `| table` a vector of maps drawn as a table, `| print` the
      literal of the answer, a string in its quotes. A failure prints as
      its alert, the error at rest `::Tag!{…}` with the subject where it
      arose cut to an excerpt, under the line
      `|~| raised, and no !| caught it` and above the prose of its
      refusal's page as line comments `|~| …`, which tells how to repair
      the call, a refused text holding a caret beneath its line, `:marker`
      under `:source`; `!| json` writes a failure as JSON. Every answer
      goes to stdout with the exit code 0, a failure among them, a query
      that does not parse answering `::quote/ParseError`, so calls run side
      by side never stop each other. A script that tests the code
      writes `--fail` before the query, `qlang --fail 'get /id' < a.json
      && next`: a failure exits with 1, written as without it. A defect
      raised past the fail track goes to stderr as `qlang:` and its
      message with the code 1, and a call that names no query or a flag
      the command line takes not goes there with its usage and the code 2.

      Every answer prints within one budget of characters, but in the
      REPL, which prints it whole, and one past it prints as
      `::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError`, whose `:budget`
      names the budget, with its head, each `::elision` in it a `:read`
      that continues the query to the part;
      `print | out` or `json | out` writes the whole, and `| elide N` cuts
      an answer on purpose. `in`, `out`, `err` and `tap` read and write the
      streams, `tap` showing a value halfway through a query on stderr as
      an answer prints; a query that writes to stdout by `out` holds the
      channel, and its answer is left unwritten. A value one query hands
      another over a pipe keeps its kinds as its literal, `print | out`
      writing it and `in | parse | open` reading it back.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::vec/table | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
      ::link~(::any/tap | doc) ::link~(::qlang/hosts | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ No value: `::cli` names the command line. ~~| #[]
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::cli` names the command line, its page and its words, and
            takes no payload: it is asked by itself, `::cli | doc`.
        ~~|
    | proves :declaresTable
        |~~ The command line declares `table` under vectors. ~~|
        ~(::vec | manifest | has ::vec/table)
    | proves :declaresTheStreams
        |~~ The command line declares the streams under any value. ~~|
        ~(::any | manifest | has ::any/out)
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAValue
        |~~ No value is put under `::cli`. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::cli !| tag | eq ::cli/PayloadKindNotTakenError)

:any/in
  |~~ Answers the text stdin holds, read to its end whatever the subject,
      the text the command line also lifts into the subject of a query
      that reads one; a terminal with nothing piped answers the empty
      string. It heads a query that reads the text as it came, for which
      the command line lifts nothing, `in | lines | count` counting the
      lines piped in and `in | parseJson` reading the text as JSON alone.
      It takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The text stdin held, the empty string when nothing was piped.
        ~~|
        ::string
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/out
  |~~ Writes a line to stdout and answers its subject as it came: it
      writes the subject when it is a string, `"hi" | out`, or the text
      the call gives, computed against the subject, `42 | out json`, the
      steps after still reading `42`. A query that writes by `out`
      leaves its answer unwritten.
      ::snippet~([1 2] | out json | count) writes `[1,2]`.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :text
        |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::string]
    | throws :text/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The text `out` writes is a string: a value of another kind is
            made text first, `out json` or `out print`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `out` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line; another
            value is written as the text the call computes from it,
            `42 | out json` or `{:x 1} | out print`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :expected |~~ The kind written alone, `#[::string]`. ~~| ::set
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the subject refused: the first frame of the trace holds the subject. ~~| ::tag)
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `out` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives,
            `"hi" | out`; texts are joined first,
            `out (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :SubjectKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no line `out` writes alone. ~~|
        ~(42 | out !| get [~(tag) ~(expected)] | eq [::any/out/SubjectKindNotTakenError #[::string]])
    | proves :text/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no text to write. ~~|
        ~("hi" | out 5 !| tag | eq ::any/out/text/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTexts
        |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~("hi" | out "a" "b" !| tag | eq ::any/out/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/err
  |~~ Writes a line to stderr, the channel of diagnostics, as `out`
      writes to stdout, and answers its subject as it came; the answer of
      the query is written as ever.
      ::snippet~([1 2 3] | err "counting" | count) writes `counting` to
      stderr and answers 3.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :text
        |~~ The text written, computed against the subject, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::string]
    | throws :text/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The text `err` writes is a string: a value of another kind is
            made text first, `err json` or `err print`.
        ~~|
    | proves :text/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A text that is no string is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | err 7 !| tag | eq ::any/err/text/KindNotTakenError)
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :SubjectKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `err` alone writes its subject, a string, as a line on stderr;
            another value is written as the text the call computes from
            it, `err json` or `err print`.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :expected |~~ The kind written alone, `#[::string]`. ~~| ::set
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the subject refused: the first frame of the trace holds the subject. ~~| ::tag)
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `err` writes its subject, or the one text its call gives,
            `"hi" | err`; texts are joined first,
            `err (["a" "b"] | join "")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :SubjectKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no line `err` writes alone. ~~|
        ~(42 | err !| get [~(tag) ~(expected)] | eq [::any/err/SubjectKindNotTakenError #[::string]])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTexts
        |~~ A call with two texts is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~("hi" | err "a" "b" !| tag | eq ::any/err/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/tap
  |~~ Writes `[tap <label>]` and its subject to stderr as an answer prints,
      the print of its kind among it, within the budget of every answer,
      and answers the subject as it came, a look at a value halfway
      through a query:
      ::snippet~([{:on true} {:on false}] | tap :before | filter /on | tap :after | count)
      shows both vectors and answers 1. A subject past the budget is
      written as `::qlang/AnswerBeyondBudgetError` with its head, each
      `:read` in it continuing the query from the subject at the `tap`,
      and `err print` writes a look whole.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :label
        |~~ The label written before the value, a keyword. ~~|
        /0 ::keyword
    | throws :label/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The label of `tap` is a keyword, `tap :before`, written before
            the value as `[tap before]`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject as it came. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `tap` writes its subject under one label its call gives,
            `5 | tap :n`; a call with more labels is refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :label/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no label. ~~|
        ~(1 | tap "x" !| tag | eq ::any/tap/label/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoLabels
        |~~ A call with two labels is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(5 | tap :a :b !| tag | eq ::any/tap/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:vec/table
  |~~ Answers a vector of maps drawn as a table of fixed-width columns, a
      text for a terminal. The columns are the keys in the order they
      first occur across the rows, and a key a row lacks draws an empty
      cell; a string cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every
      other value as its literal on one line, a doc with each line break
      of its prose written as a space. The empty vector draws as
      `(empty)`. A single map goes in a vector first, `get [~(it)] | table`, and
      the call takes no value.
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The rows drawn as text, a line each. ~~| ::string
    | throws :SubjectNotVecError
        |~~ `table` draws a vector of maps; a single map goes in a vector
            first, `{:name "alice"} | get [~(it)] | table`.
        ~~|
    | throws :RowKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Every row of `table` is a map, its keys the columns; the path
            of its frame names the first that is not.
        ~~|
        ~(wires :expected |~~ The kind of a row, `#[::map]`. ~~| ::set
          | wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the row refused. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The row refused. ~~|)
    | proves :drawsEachRow
        |~~ Each row is drawn. ~~|
        ~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")
    | proves :drawsANestedValueAsItsLiteral
        |~~ A nested value is drawn as its literal. ~~|
        ~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")
    | proves :drawsNothingAsEmpty
        |~~ An empty vector draws as `(empty)`. ~~|
        ~([] | table | eq "(empty)")
    | proves :drawsAMapInAVector
        |~~ A single map is drawn in a vector. ~~|
        ~({:name "alice"} | get [~(it)] | table | contains "alice")
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A single map is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~({:name "alice"} | table !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::map])
    | proves :SubjectNotVecError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector of rows. ~~|
        ~(42 | table !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::vec/table/SubjectNotVecError ::number])
    | proves :RowKindNotTakenError/refusesARowThatIsNoMap
        |~~ A row that is no map is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~([42 "hello"] | table !| get [~(tag) ~(trace | last | path) ~(actualTag) ~(expected)]
          | eq [::vec/table/RowKindNotTakenError /0 ::number #[::map]])
    | impl ::builtin{}

